n the hundred and first had done well  demonstrating competence and  professionalism capably led they took  hardship in stride and refused to let  bloodlust cynicism or other despoilers  of good armies cheat them of their  battle honors and I add this personal  judgment which readers are welcome to  take or leave it's neither right nor  wrong it's a judgement the soldiers were  better than the cause they served I do  believe we all should be concerned about  whether the US Army is excessively  stretched and stressed before September  11 2001 roughly 20% of the active duty  the United States Army was deployed  overseas two years later the figure had  climbed to nearly half 10,000 soldiers  in Afghanistan five thousand in the  Balkans thirty-seven thousand in South  Korea 120 thousand in Iraq and troops  scattered in more than 110 other  countries last June at his retirement  ceremony the Army's outgoing chief of  staff general Eric Shinseki warned  beware the 12 division strategy for a10  division army and no less strained as  many of you here know or the National  Guard and the reserves there are many  tens of thousands of Guardsmen and  reservists now on active duty worldwide  and a sign that you sometimes see in  reserve bivouacs and I saw it in Iraq a  couple times observes one weekend a  month my ass  we have a responsibility to own the army  jointly it's not President Bush's army  any more than it was President Clinton's  army it is our army and one of the acts  of citizenship should be to take a  vested interest in the faith of our army  we hang with it or we hang together  without it and as a final political note  I would only recall Machiavelli's words  wars begin where you will but they do  not end where you please well on the  deepest level my ambition in writing  about war is to transcend the  particulars of time place and even  character to approach something that's  timeless and placeless to convey an  emotional truth and that I suppose is a  truth that