ve to  him the writing of history is not a  science but distinctly one of the  humanities and only those with deep  feelings for the human predicament  should try it I'd like to close before  we open it to questions by introducing  you to a single soldier who is  emblematic of soldiers in an age when  wars are small sequential expeditionary  and bottomless this is from the prologue  of in the company of soldiers they found  the sergeant's body at mid-morning on  Saturday April 12th 2003 just where an  Iraqi boy said it would be  in a shallow grave in South Baghdad near  the highway 8 cloverleaf known to the US  Army as objective Curley his interment  was imperfect an elbow and a knee  protruded from the covering rubble he  had been stripped of boots and combat  gear but not his uniform and his rank  stripes and the name tape sewn over his  right breast pocket made identification  easy sergeant first class John W  Marshall who had been missing since  Iraqi forces ambushed his convoy below  Curley on April 8th a rocket-propelled  grenade had ruined sergeant Marshalls  back and arm for days in the ground had  spoiled the rest of him soldiers from  the hundred and first Airborne Division  recorded the map grid of his makeshift  burial plot MB four four nine six two  seven five two nine five and a chaplain  read from Psalms by the time I arrived  at the site the remains had been lifted  into a body bag draped with an American  American flag and carried headfirst as  prescribed by Army custom to a Humvee a  graves registration team took the body  for eventual burial in Arlington  National Cemetery I learned more about  sergeant Marshall in the coming weeks it  was fifty years old making him the  senior American soldier killed in the  war it served in the third Battalion of  the 15th Infantry Regiment a legendary  unit in the 3rd Infantry Division and he  died while firing a mark-19 automatic  grenade launcher at marauding Iraqi  paramilitaries the fatal RPG round had  blown him from his Humvee turret and in  the chaos of combat his corpse had been  left behind  born in Los Angeles Marshall had joined  the army at 18 his father Joseph was an  army quartermaster during World War two  his mother  I have been a medical technician in the  Women's Auxiliary Army auxilary Corps an  unusual distinction for a black woman in  those days  Odessa Marshall would wear her uniform  to her son's funeral  sergeant Marshall had left the Army for  four years in the 1980s in a successful  fight against Hodgkin's lymphoma with  the cancer in remission he rejoined the  service the war in Iraq was his first  combat tour and he was nearing  retirement his survivors included a  widow Denise and six children ages 9 to  17 they collected his posthumous Bronze  Star and his Purple Heart in a political  democracy every soldier's death is a  public event every soldier's death ought  to provoke the hard question why did he  die even without having met sergeant  Marshall I could surmise that he had his  own answers his rank indicated enough  time and service to have sorted out such  existential issues later I would learn  that in his last dispatch home he saw a  little merit and debating the mission in  Iraq it's really not an issue with me he  wrote I'm not a politician or a  policymaker  just an old soldier any doubts on my  part could get someone killed but  private rationales  however valid and honorable rarely  satisfied public inquiries why did  sergeant Marshall die if you find  yourself ruminating on that question as  I hope you do and I think we must then  you too are in the company of soldiers  thank you very much I look forward to  your questions  [Music]  [Applause]  [Music]  harren raise your hand to raise your  hand again here there we go  I'd like to ask you about the embedding  of journalists with combat units do you  think that it was because of the  undeniable compelling nature of the  stories of the boots on the ground that  it obscured the larger picture of what  was going on and if so do you think that  was by intent well it's a good question  and it's an issue that is debated in  journalistic circles intently a year  later and it's debated in the military -  there were 777 embedded journalists 20%  of them incidentally were not American  citizens about 500 and some of us  crossed into Iraq the stated intent from  the Pentagon was to allow reporters to  have an inside view of what units were  doing in a way that had not been done  particularly during the Persian Gulf War  of 1991 when I think almost no one was  satisfied with coverage my personal  feeling and embedding was not a new  concept the third time I went to Somalia  I was embedded with the Marines had been  embedded with the 1st Armored Division  in Bosnia but my own personal feeling  was that if someone who's been around  the army all my life my father was an  infantry officer I've reported on the  army and the other services  professionally for many years this was  an a uniquely intimate view I've never  seen the inside of the army the way I  saw it this time there are hazards to  embedding there's no doubt about it one  of the hazards is the danger of being  co-opted it's something that reporters  need to watch out for at something that  is not unique to covering the hundred  and first Airborne Division you can be  co-opted if you are in the company of  people you admire and people with whom  you share a hardship people in this case  and musi died I think it's possible  possible to be co-opted if in fact  you're covering the Tennessee Titans or  you're covering the Louisville City  Council  it's one of the things that  distinguishes good reporters from less  good reporters it's the capacity to  maintain some distance and the requisite  objectivity to write about it warts and  all even when it hurts as for not seeing  the larger strategic issues I was  looking at the world through a soda  straw and I had no illusions that I was  seeing anything other than what I could  see essentially through general  Petraeus's eyes and the eyes of his  subordinate commanders and his soldiers  I think that if there were deficiencies  and covering the larger strategic issues  and I'll stipulate that there were there  always are in part it's because when  you've got 777 reporters out there they  tend to slice and dice the world into  very narrow strips and it's difficult to  put it together into a large panorama  was it intentional by the Pentagon I  don't think it was to be honest with you  I think what the Pentagon was trying to  do I know what the Pentagon was trying  to do in part was to have what they  thought were honest witnesses out there  to bear witness in case the Iraqis  claimed that there were atrocities by  the invading army as well as to provide  a conduit back home to the families  waiting and I know that that was quite  effective I think I heard a lot from the  families of the hundred and first that  they liked hearing about what their  soldiers were doing and in that case I  think it was successful mr. Atkinson  I've never served in the Army but I'm an  army daughter an army wife an army  sister and an army mother so I do  believe I have served  there's no pension is he no there isn't  my question to you is more of a  philosophical one the premise of the  professional soldier is to train for war  but if you say that war is indeed the  most corrosive thing to the human spirit  how do we reconcile this difference in  the fact that men and women choose a  career that is ultimately so corrosive  if they do have to act  in their capacity as a trained  professional well that's a good question  and how do you square that circle you  know first of all I think the profession  of arms is a noble calling and I think  it is a calling it's not for everyone as  someone once said it's like the church  it's not for everyone and so again I  stress that I do not suggest for a  moment that the sacrifice and the  demands on being a professional soldier  or even a soldier who serves one term  and goes on to college or whatever is in  any way diminished by this but I think  it's important to recognize that the  nature of war and I'm not just talking  about the war in Iraq in 2003 I lived in  World War two everyday I know I wrote a  check the other day and I dated it  January 10 1944 that's either a good  sign or a very bad sign  and the kind of corrosion that I'm  talking about this this this attack on  the spirit is something that is endemic  to all wars probably going back to the  Peloponnesian wars and before you  reconcile it I think by acknowledging  the need for an army and I'm talking  about all the services here of course  the fact that they protect us the fact  that they provide the bulwark against  assaults on our freedom the fact that  they allow us to enjoy the quality of  life that we've got and at the same time  when they go off to war first of all you  have a responsibility I think to make  sure that it's the right war and that  the sacrifices both the physical  sacrifices and the spiritual sacrifices  are in fact commensurate with the gains  I don't think you can do more than that  as a people it's part of the body  politic it's it's part of how we operate  as a people and have for 230 years or so  so you know I don't have a magic  rhetorical bullet to make make it go  away I'm just telling you it's there and  it's something we should be aware of  thanks for the good question mr.  Atkinson at the beginning you asked how  many people had served in the armed  services I would like for you to ask the  question now as to how many people here  have someone presently serving in their  family and their services and then  comment on those numbers as compared to  the prior numbers okay if you heard a  question how many of you have family  members serving in the military now  would you raise your hands well it's a  whole lot it's a little hard to see up  here in the lights I can't tell whether  the first raising of hands was larger  than the second you know the point of  fact is that we are in amazingly  considering that we do not have a dry  armie we do not have sons and daughters  as two generations ago who were in  certain jeopardy of being drafted into  the army where they wanted to go or not  but yet we're invested as a people the  hundreds of folks in this room either  have direct experience have served  directly or you've got loved ones who  are serving and you know the burden and  the sacrifice that I'm talking about I  don't know how to comment more than that  other than to say that every time I talk  to people all around the country and  particularly when I talk to young people  on college campuses and so on where  they're generally not thinking about  going into the army of the Navy of the  Air Force and the Marines I try to  remind them that there are people just  like you just like you I have a 20 year  old son and an 18 year old daughter and  they aren't going in the military and I  preach to them there are lots of people  just like you who are making tremendous  sacrifices on your behalf don't forget  it  that's all you can ask mr. Atkins from  the last time you were here you  commented on how the Army in World War  two was equipped and I was wondering  what your view was of our army in Iraq  and it's equipment the army in general  in Iraq in 2003 and today in 2004 is  immensely superior to the army that went  into North Africa say on November 8 1942  its superior in its training its  superior in its education level its  superior in its preparations across the  board and it is way superior in terms of  equipment it is like an army from a  different planet today compared to that  army in 1942 there are lot of things  that are familiar to a soldier from 1942  if you were looking at an infantryman  today for example he would recognize the  infantry waddle he'd recognized the  concerns of an infantryman he would not  recognize  to a large extent the the equipment that  he's got and there were things that in  this recent war were not even available  in the Persian Gulf War in 1991 I'll  give you one example and it was a  remarkable thing to watch the military  developed something called blue force  tracker and blue force tracker is a  system that allows you to put small  electronic devices that emit a signal  and you can put them on tanks you can  put them on Humvees you can put them on  armored personnel carriers you can put  them on helicopters and they transmit a  signal to a satellite and it is back to  a receiver which can display all these  blue icons of where these individual  vehicles are on a screen so the general  Patraeus sitting in his Tactical  Operations Center could see on this  screen where such-and-such a platoon was  where such-and-such a company or  battalion was it provides astonishing  situational awareness to use the  military term it's one of the reasons  why there wasn't more fratricide in this  conflict and it's one of many  developments from the kind of rucksacks  the soldiers carry right up to the kind  of munitions and that the predominance  of precision munitions today even  compared to twelve years ago in the  Persian Gulf War so I think we're  treating them pretty well in terms of  the equipment that you got soldiers tend  soldiers that I talked to and was with  tended to be quite happy with the kit as  the British call it you may address this  in the book I don't know but in the  papers following it reaching Baghdad and  so forth the general Wallace the corps  commander whom he spoke of was quoted as  having said something to the effect and  I guess the the post back there there  that they didn't prepare us for this or  I don't know the exact words but having  been close to the command there and him  I suppose I wondered what whether there  was reaction to that by the yeah I know  exactly what he said sir because he said  it to me and we weren't in Baghdad yet  it was still fairly early in the war it  was roughly March 28th and it was during  the terrible sandstorm the three-day  sandstorm that you'll recall when the  army and its drive toward Baghdad had  essentially stalled as a consequence of  the conditions and the conditions  included marauding attacks by Iraqis on  the supply lines that had not been  anticipated there was a belief that when  the Army and the Marines blew into Iraq  that the Shiite majority in the southern  part of Iraq would welcome them as  liberators expect parades was the phrase  that came from Washington and in fact  that didn't happen initially they were  greeted with gunfire and there was a  change of plan no plan survives contact  with the enemy proverbially and they had  to subdue these cities general Wallace  showed up at at Forward Operating Base  shell to see General Petraeus and I  asked general Wallace after he had  conferred with Petraeus how he felt  things were going basically and he said  the enemy were fighting is is different  from the enemy we war gamed against and  he elaborated on that a bit and what he  meant was we didn't anticipate these  suicidal attacks from the Fed again the  Iraqi diehards the bath party members  attacking out of cities and using the  cities of sanctuary threatening this  very thin thread of a supply line that  stretched all the way back to Kuwait how  was it received it was received very  poorly frankly  by the White House in particular general  Wallace whom I adore is a very competent  combat commander and a real straight  talker and you ask him a question and he  will give you a straight answer and this  caused all kinds of heartburn in  Washington where they were running  around with their hair on fire  mainly because it was at odds with the  assertions out of the Pentagon in  particular and out of the White House to  some extent that everything was going  swimmingly and that in fact we were we  were virtually in Baghdad and the war  was going to be over soon and it from  the field it just didn't look that way  Wallace essentially was it was suggested  to him that he hold his tongue he did he  rejiggered his plan and I think  ultimately felt that what he had said  was a bit of a tempest in a teapot I saw  him in August and we both agreed that we  were puzzled by the storm that this  kicked up he's currently the commander  at Fort Leavenworth and he remains a  great soldier  [Applause]  [Music]  [Music]  Rick Atkins in his author of an army at  dawn crusade and the long gray line his  latest in the company of soldiers is  number eight on this week's New York  Times nonfiction bestsellers list it's  published by Henry Holt on the web at  Henry Holt com  [Music]
 

